```c
#include <stdio.h>

/**
 * @brief Sorts an integer array using the bubble sort algorithm.
 *
 * @param arr The integer array to be sorted.
 * @param size The number of elements in the array.
 */
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int size) {
    int i, j;
    int temp;
    int swapped;

    // Outer loop for passes through the array
    for (i = 0; i < size - 1; i++) {
        swapped = 0; // Flag to optimize: if no two elements were swapped by inner loop, the array is sorted

        // Inner loop for comparisons and swaps
        // The last i elements are already in place, so we only need to go up to size - 1 - i
        for (j = 0; j < size - 1 - i; j++) {
            // Compare adjacent elements
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                // Swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
                temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
                swapped = 1;
            }
        }

        // If no two elements were swapped by inner loop, then the array is sorted
        if (swapped == 0) {
            break;
        }
    }
}

/**
 * @brief Prints the elements of an integer array.
 *
 * @param arr The integer array to print.
 * @param size The number of elements in the array.
 */
void printArray(int arr[], int size) {
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}

// Example usage of the bubbleSort function
int main() {
    int numbers[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
    int n = sizeof(numbers) / sizeof(numbers[0]);

    printf("Original array: \n");
    printArray(numbers, n);

    // Call the sorting function
    bubbleSort(numbers, n);

    printf("\nSorted array (using Bubble Sort): \n");
    printArray(numbers, n);

    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation of the Code:

1.  **`void bubbleSort(int arr[], int size)`**:
    *   This is the core function that takes the array (`arr`) and its size (`size`) as input.
    *   It uses two nested loops to compare and swap elements.

2.  **Outer Loop (`for (i = 0; i < size - 1; i++)`)**:
    *   This loop controls the number of passes required. After each pass, the largest unsorted element "bubbles up" to its correct final position at the end of the unsorted portion of the array.
    *   We need `size - 1` passes because if `size - 1` elements are sorted, the last one must also be in place.

